    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Tada Silver Mine, opened in the 11th century, produced silver and copper and reached peak output in the 17th century, making Kawanishi a tenryō territory Tokugawa rule.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kawanishi%2C_Hy%C5%8Dgo"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1884, Mitsubishi established Japan's first noted water plant in Kawanishi, which later became the famous Mitsuya Cider brand.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kawanishi%2C_Hy%C5%8Dgo"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hankyu's Kawanishi-Noseguchi Station serves as a transfer hub to the Nose Railway, which runs primarily within Kawanishi.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kawanishi%2C_Hy%C5%8Dgo"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kawanishi lies directly east of the Inagawa River, which forms its western boundary.",
